063" :question/id #uuid "5ca0b50b-5d4d-532a-8bbd-aad24e29bc44" :question/math-academy-id "q-332063" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{8t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{8t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332063/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332063"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331791/field-1/e7491a7dbd8813c76dac9bfb287cbbb08b8503b948c9a19fe8b4760b37edc06a" :answer/id #uuid "de14a5a6-7de0-56bc-8d3c-8179fcf8dd03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}y(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331791/field-1/5b992ba4b697e8be8ddf1d0afd2ef9779c3d607946a9c27cd9ea4ae9a6f95826" :answer/id #uuid "6586df48-7d42-5f98-a6e5-5b349877726f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{4t}y(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331791/field-1/d806873fe1158d3ba1e6610fbf5b53a55f152a248679db124bf71c9a1204ffe9" :answer/id #uuid "bd931314-5d88-5b8d-9697-eaed9b459f27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}y(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331791/field-1/b3e1055d91438940bfa194f1b44d59184c06fc9e4afe9ddb540b9af54e45af60" :answer/id #uuid "1d15be57-10d4-58cd-9d81-9ac2ef61c7ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value