#uuid "10e2d5d1-9842-5c07-9fc8-53352a9d569e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "G(s+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-3/d263db7614df608879c942b690305df5d3d30385a1743a1486f1af1ab837d16f" :answer/id #uuid "ebaed1bd-fd69-5db3-bb01-8f4d83efd9f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "G(s-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-3/81157f56b7a5af4ed0f8b11a103dc05fa76b3a05a90487ea6104c1f6f5638c2f" :answer/id #uuid "1500588d-4616-571b-9fbf-dd6a9a5999e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "G(s)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "25e5f736-3a03-595d-96e3-131f5035de32" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-331788/field-3/1f033588b254a8d91e506acf8088d1656ff012d34b5a64ab8c370c3e4bfa7ae6" :answer-field/choices ["q-331788/field-3/1f033588b254a8d91e506acf8088d1656ff012d34b5a64ab8c370c3e4bfa7ae6" "q-331788/field-3/d263db7614df608879c942b690305df5d3d30385a1743a1486f1af1ab837d16f" "q-331788/field-3/81157f56b7a5af4ed0f8b11a103dc05fa76b3a05a90487ea6104c1f6f5638c2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-4/09e8ca37212e0e2dd02285849329c6d748dd9b5a8088d6c675d59589537e7a41" :answer/id #uuid "393a0ab1-8b61-50be-8060-8f3edad3d7eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-4/2b99c00fa354699b9b03e10646c24e2d9809ea5d273483202e1f7a139da4bdeb" :answer/id #uuid "5d11f90c-f699-5249-a9ad-d9b6a5b1af18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-4/48b411620ed377ee9222b817c8ed23aa5c39bdb48880d81d507d29eeec3ddf84" :answer/id #uuid "34082b47-4677-5863-825a-62ab305eafa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d028e17-e3d4-5acb-9123-151988d70dd8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-331788/field-4/2b99c00fa354699b9b03e10646c24e2d9809ea5d273483202e1f7a139da4bdeb" :answer-field/choices ["q-331788/field-4/09e8ca37212e0e2dd02285849329c6d748dd9b5a8088d6c675d59589537e7a41" "q-331788/field-4/2b99c00fa354699b9b03e10646c24e2d9809ea5d273483202e1f7a139da4bdeb" "q-331788/field-4/48b411620ed377ee9222b817c8ed23aa5c39bdb48880d81d507d29eeec3ddf84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331788" :question/id #uuid "66afff54-1f60-5120-ada5-9744f4e97fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-331788" :question/problem "Consider a function $g(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{g(t)}=G(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{-3t}g(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}g(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}g(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+3)t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $G(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+3)t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=G(s+3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331788/field-1" "q-331788/field-2" "q-331788/field-3" "q-331788/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331788"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21424"] :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21590"] :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{2{t}^{2}}+L{5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21549"] :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a < 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21531"] :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0.$ Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}.$ Show the steps to compute this transform." :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-7t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
